TITLE:: MENote
summary:: MENote object for MENoteRange.
categories:: MusicEngine Library
related:: Classes/MENoteRange, Classes/MERange, Classes/MERegister, Reference/RangeSymbols

DESCRIPTION::
MENote encapsulates all note data (MIDI, frequency, degree, name, etc.) into a single object.

NOTE:: MENote objects are not designed to be instantiated directly by the user, but to be instantiated internally when generating a MENoteRange. The user may then access them, via the MENoteRange object, and retrieve data using the methods described in this file.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7"); // MENoteRange[C#-1:P5, E-1:m7, F#-1:P1, A-1:m3, ...]
r.size;      // A 43 MENote collection, ranging from octaves -1 to 9
r[30].name   // F#6
r[30].midi   // 90
r[30].degree // P1
::
::


CLASSMETHODS::


METHOD:: new

ARGUMENT:: noteLetter
Letter to be used when generating a note name.

ARGUMENT:: midiNote
MIDI note number.

ARGUMENT:: interval
Interval symbol (e.g.: CODE::\P5::) relative to a root.

ARGUMENT:: data
User defined data dictionary. If none is provided, an empty dictionary will be assigned to the new CODE::MENote:: object.

ARGUMENT:: validate
Used for debugging only. When set to CODE::true::, validate activates validator methods that test input validity throughout the process of creating a CODE::MENote:: instance.

returns:: A new CODE::MENote:: object.

CODE::
n = MENote.new("C", 60, \P1);

n.name;   // C4
n.degree; // P1
n.midi;   // 60
n.freq;   // 261.62Hz
::


METHOD:: newFromName

ARGUMENT:: noteName
String representing the note name (e.g., EMPHASIS::C#4::). String must contain letter (EMPHASIS::A-G::), zero to 5 sharps or flats, and octave number.

ARGUMENT:: interval
Interval symbol (e.g.: CODE::\P5::) relative to a root.

ARGUMENT:: data
User defined data dictionary. If none is provided, an empty dictionary will be assigned to the new CODE::MENote:: object.

returns:: A new CODE::MENote:: object


INSTANCEMETHODS::


SUBSECTION:: Pitch data


METHOD:: midi
Returns a MIDI note number.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[10].midi; // 30
::


METHOD:: freq
Returns a frequency value.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[10].freq; // 46.24Hz
::


SUBSECTION:: Name data


METHOD:: name
Returns a note name in scientific notation (EMPHASIS::A::-EMPHASIS::G::). The argument EMPHASIS::charSet:: should be of type symbol, and allows the user to choose between different character sets to represent the accidental sign. The argument EMPHASIS::withOctave:: is a boolean flag that determines whether an octave number should be appended to the string.

NOTE:: For the argument EMPHASIS::charSet::, the following values are accepted:
LIST::
## CODE::\ascii:: for ASCII characters (# and b),
## CODE::\unic:: for Unicode characters (𝄪, ♯, ♭ and 𝄫),
## CODE::\m21:: for a format compatible with the Python's module music21 (# and -).
::
::

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[10].name;               // F#1
r[10].name(\unic, false); // F♯
::


METHOD:: sol
Similar to CODE::name::, but uses the fixed-do Solfège naming convention.

NOTE:: For the argument EMPHASIS::charSet::, the following values are accepted:
LIST::
## CODE::\ascii:: for ASCII characters (# and b),
## CODE::\unic:: for Unicode characters (𝄪, ♯, ♭ and 𝄫),
## CODE::\m21:: for a format compatible with the Python's module music21 (# and -).
::
::

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[10].sol;               // Fa#1
r[10].sol(\unic, false); // Fa♯
::


METHOD:: letter
Returns the letter representing the note name.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[10].letter; // F
::


METHOD:: octave
Returns an octave number.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[10].octave; // 1
::


METHOD:: sign
Returns an accidental sign.

NOTE:: For the argument EMPHASIS::charSet::, the following values are accepted:
LIST::
## CODE::\ascii:: for ASCII characters (# and b),
## CODE::\unic:: for Unicode characters (𝄪, ♯, ♭ and 𝄫),
## CODE::\m21:: for a format compatible with the Python's module music21 (# and -).
::
::

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[10].sign;        // #
r[10].sign(\unic); // ♯
::


METHOD:: signOffset
Returns an integer representation of the accidental sign.

NOTE::
Sign offsets range from -5 (CODE::bbbbb::) to 5 (CODE::#####::). An offset of 0 means the note has no accidental.
::

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[10].signOffset; // 1
::


SUBSECTION:: Interval data


METHOD:: interval
Returns a MEInterval object associated with the MENote.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[10].interval; // MEInterval(P1)
::


METHOD:: degree
Returns a symbol representation of the degree, or CODE::nil:: if no interval information is available. It is possible to assign an interval symbol directly to a MENote.

NOTE::
When a range is instantiated via the CODE::newFromName:: method, degree information must be directly assigned by the user, otherwise the CODE::degree:: property, in each MENote, will be set to CODE::nil::. See LINK::Classes/MENoteRange#*newFromName::, for more information.
::

CODE::
n = MENote.newFromName("Bb4");
n.degree;       // nil
n.interval;     // nil
n.number;       // nil
n.quality;      // nil

n.degree = \P5;
n.degree;       // P5
n.interval;     // MEInterval(P5)
n.number;       // 5.2
n.quality;      // P

// Note ranges instantiated with new always contain degree information
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[10].degree; // P1
::


METHOD:: number
Returns a numeric representation of the degree. By default a float is returned, where the whole part represents interval number and the decimal part represents the quality. If EMPHASIS::asInt:: is set to CODE::true::, only the whole part is returned as an integer.

NOTE::
When representing interval quality as decimal values, the following mapping is applied: CODE::0.0=\d, 0.1=\m, 0.2=\P, 0.3=\M, 0.4=\A::.
::

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[10].number;       // 1.2 (P1)
r[10].number(true); // 1
::


METHOD:: quality
Returns the quality of the degree as a symbol. If EMPHASIS::asFloat:: is set to CODE::true::, then a float representation is returned.

NOTE::
When representing interval quality as decimal values, the following mapping is applied: CODE::0.0=\d, 0.1=\m, 0.2=\P, 0.3=\M, 0.4=\A::.
::

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[10].quality;       // P
r[10].quality(true); // 0.2
::


SUBSECTION:: User-assigned data


METHOD:: data
Returns a dictionary used for user-assigned data.

NOTE::
During instantiation, if no dictionary is passed, an empty one is assigned to the MENote object.
::

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[0].set(\dur, 1);
r[0].set(\amp, 0.5);

r[0].data; // Dictionary[(dur -> 1), (amp -> 0.5)]
::


METHOD:: set
Allows the user to register a EMPHASIS::value:: associated with a EMPHASIS::key::.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[0].set(\dur, 0.5);

r[0].data; // Dictionary[(\dur -> 0.5)]
::


METHOD:: get
Allows the user to retrieve the value associated with the respective EMPHASIS::key::.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[0].set(\dur, 0.5);
r[0].get(\dur);      // 0.5

// Same as:
r[0].data[\dur];     // 0.5
::


METHOD:: clearValue
Allows the user to clear the value associated with the respective EMPHASIS::key::.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[0].set(\dur, 0.5);
r[0].get(\dur);        // 0.5
r[0].clearValue(\dur);
r[0].get(\dur);        // nil
::


METHOD:: clearDict
Allows the user to clear all data in the dictionary.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[0].set(\dur, 0.5);
r[0].set(\amp, 0.3);
r[0].get(\dur);      // 0.5
r[0].get(\amp);      // 0.3
r[0].clearDict;
r[0].data.isEmpty;   // true
::


METHOD:: getKeys
Returns a set with all the keys in the dictionary.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[0].set(\dur, 0.5);
r[0].set(\amp, 0.3);
r[0].getKeys;        // Set[amp, dur]
::


METHOD:: printData
Prints all entries in the dictionary.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[0].set(\dur, 0.5);
r[0].set(\amp, 0.3);
r[0].printData;
::


SUBSECTION:: Note transposition


CODE::MENote:: transposition may be achieved using an integer value, or a symbol representing the interval by which the CODE::MENote:: is to be transposed. When transposed, note names are changed accordingly.

Symbols allow transpositions up to a 14th (CODE::\A1:: to CODE::\M14::), while the shift of the MIDI value does not go beyond MATH::\pm 11::. Using symbols also allows for greater control over what the transposed note name will be.

Integers allow any leap provided the transposed MIDI value does not exceed 127. Integers default to the following intervals:

TABLE::
 ##0 || CODE::\P1::
 ##1 || CODE::\m2::
 ##2 || CODE::\M2::
 ##3 || CODE::\m3::
 ##4 || CODE::\M3::
 ##5 || CODE::\P4::
 ##6 || CODE::\A4::
 ##7 || CODE::\P5::
 ##8 || CODE::\m6::
 ##9 || CODE::\M6::
 ##10 || CODE::\m7::
 ##11 || CODE::\M7::
::


METHOD:: transposeUp
Returns a new MENote with its pitch data and name transposed up, by EMPHASIS::interval::. If the transposed MIDI value MATH::>127::, the method returns CODE::nil::.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange("F#-7");
r[10];                      // F#1:P1
n = r[10].transposeUp(\d5); // C2:P1
m = r[10].transposeUp(6);   // B#1:P1

r[10].midi;                 // 30
n.midi;                     // 36
m.midi;                     // 36

// Transposition over an octave
o = r[10].transposeUp(24);  // Two octaves higher

r[10].midi;                 // 30
o.midi;                     // 54
::


METHOD:: transposeDown
Returns a new MENote with its pitch data and name transposed down, by EMPHASIS::interval::. If the transposed MIDI value MATH::<0::, the method returns CODE::nil::.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange("F#-7");
r[10];                        // F#1:P1
n = r[10].transposeDown(\d5); // B#0:P1
m = r[10].transposeDown(6);   // C1:P1

r[10].midi;                   // 30
n.midi;                       // 24
m.midi;                       // 24

// Transposition over an octave
o = r[10].transposeDown(24);  // Two octaves down

r[10].midi;                   // 30
o.midi;                       // 6
::


METHOD:: >>
Shorthand for CODE::transposeUp::.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange("F#-7");

r[10];            // F#1:P1
n = r[10] >> \d5; // C2:P1
m = r[10] >> 6;   // B#1:P1

r[10].midi;       // 30
n.midi;           // 36
m.midi;           // 36
::


METHOD:: <<
Shorthand for CODE::transposeDown::.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange("F#-7");

r[10];            // F#1:P1
n = r[10] << \d5; // B#0:P1
m = r[10] << 6;   // C1:P1

r[10].midi;       // 30
n.midi;           // 24
m.midi;           // 24
::


SUBSECTION:: Other methods


METHOD:: hash
Returns an unique hash value for the MENote object.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[0].hash; // Some integer value
::


METHOD:: species
Returns the name of the class. Similar to the CODE::class:: method.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[0].species; // MENote
r[0].class    // MENote
::


METHOD:: copy
Returns a shallow copy of the object.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

n = r[0].copy;

n == r[0];     // true
n === r[0];    // true
::


METHOD:: deepCopy
Returns a deep copy of the object.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

n = r[0].deepCopy;

n == r[0];     // true
n === r[0];    // false
::


SUBSECTION:: Comparison operators


METHOD:: ==
Checks for equality between two MENote objects.

NOTE::
Equality is determined by the two objects having the same name and MIDI value.
::

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");
n = MENote.new("C", 1); // C#-1

r[0] == n; // true
::


METHOD:: <
Compares the MIDI values of two MENote objects to determine if left side MENote is lesser than EMPHASIS::aMENote::.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[0] < r[1]; // true  (1 < 3)
r[1] < r[0]; // false (3 < 1)
::


METHOD:: >
Compares the MIDI values of two MENote objects to determine if left side MENote is greater than EMPHASIS::aMENote::.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");

r[0] > r[1]; // false (1 > 3)
r[1] > r[0]; // true  (3 > 1)
::


METHOD:: <=
Compares two MENote objects to determine if left side MENote is lesser than or equal to EMPHASIS::aMENote::.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");
n = MENote.new("C", 1);

r[0] <= n;    // true
r[0] <= r[1]; // true  (1 <= 3)
r[1] <= r[0]; // false (3 <= 1)
::


METHOD:: >=
Compares two MENote objects to determine if left side MENote is greater than or equal to EMPHASIS::aMENote::.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");
n = MENote.new("C", 1);

r[0] >= n;    // true
r[0] >= r[1]; // false (1 >= 3)
r[1] >= r[0]; // true  (3 >= 1)
::


SUBSECTION:: Enharmony operations


METHOD:: enharmonicTo
Compares two MENote objects to determine if left side MENote has MIDI value equal to EMPHASIS::aMENote::.

NOTE::
The fundamental difference from the equality operator is that only the MIDI value gets compared.
::

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");
n = MENote.new("D", 1); // Db-1
m = MENote.new("C", 1); // C#-1

r[0].enharmonicTo(n);   // true : (Db-1, C#-1)
r[0].enharmonicTo(m);   // true : (C#-1, C#-1)
::


METHOD:: notEnharmonicTo
Compares two MENote objects to determine if left side MENote has MIDI value different from EMPHASIS::aMENote::.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");
n = MENote.new("C", 0); // C-1

r[0].notEnharmonicTo(n); // true  : (C#-1, C-1)
r[0].enharmonicTo(n);    // false : (C#-1, C-1)
::


METHOD:: ==?
Shorthand for the CODE::enharmonicTo:: method.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");
n = MENote.new("D", 1); // Db-1

r[0] ==? n; // true

// Same as;
r[0].enharmonicTo(n); // true
::


METHOD:: !=?
Shorthand for the CODE::notEnharmonicTo:: method.

CODE::
r = MENoteRange.new("F#-7");
n = MENote.new("C", 0); // C-1

r[0] !=? n; // true

// Same as;
r[0].notEnharmonicTo(n); // true
::